# /etc/bd/deploy.env.example - DOCUMENTATION ONLY. Nothing here is real and
# nothing here is loaded at boot. It documents the DEPLOY-TIME injection
# point that PRD "Secret hygiene" requires: the case id and the ONE-TIME
# registration token arrive in Vultr user_data when the twin is created,
# never at build time. user_data is base64(JSON) with exactly:
# {"case_id":"...","token":"...","control_plane":"http://VPC-IP:8000"}
# No Vultr API key and no inference key ever exists on this ISO - both stay
# on the control plane; the twin holds only the one-time token.
#
# At boot, /etc/bd/boot.sh reads the case parameters from:
#
#   1. the kernel cmdline bd.* args for non-secret network/device config:
#      bd.subnet=<case VPC subnet>
#      bd.vpc_ip=<this twin's VPC address> bd.disk=<cloned disk device>
#
#   2. a bd-deploy.env file at the root of ANY attached block device (the
#      deploy step writes one when boot args are not available). The
#      initramfs mounts every candidate device read-only, copies the file to
#      /run/bd/deploy.env and unmounts; boot.sh sources it. Same variable
#      names and shell format as this file. Cmdline args win over the file.
#
# The values below are RFC 5737 documentation addresses and obvious
# placeholders. Use this file only as the template for what the deploy step
# writes - never put real values in the repository.

BD_SUBNET=192.0.2.0/24
BD_VPC_IP=192.0.2.20
BD_DISK=/dev/vdb
